<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Morris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Morris was an English textile designer, poet, artist, writer and socialist activist associated with the British Arts and Crafts movement and the revival of traditional British textile arts. In 1861 he founded the decorative arts firm Morris, Marshall, Faulkner &amp; Co. with Edward Burne-Jones, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> Gabriel Rossetti, Philip Webb and others, designing tapestries, wallpaper, fabrics, furniture and stained glass; from 1875 it was Morris &amp; Co. His poems and novels, among them the utopian News from Nowhere (1890), helped to establish the modern fantasy genre. He founded the Society for the Protection of Ancient Buildings in 1877, the Socialist League in 1884 and the Kelmscott Press in 1891.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-morris.jpg" alt="William Morris" width="330" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frederick Hollyer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Morris_age_53.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Just as important was the English designer <b>William Morris</b>, who had argued that art should meet the needs of society.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bauhaus">Bauhaus · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Later a “Back-to-Nature” movement, advocated by John Ruskin, <b>William Morris</b>, George Bernard Shaw and Edward Carpenter, opposed consumerism and pollution and reacted against the cramped, polluted industrial towns by idealising rural life; Ruskin imagined a “small piece of English ground” with “no steam engines upon it”.</blockquote><a href="/movement/environmental-movement">Environmental movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Christian and Islamic socialism, guild socialism, syndicalism, socialist feminism and eco-socialism, which credits <b>William Morris</b> with its early principles, each took the ideas their own way.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/socialism">Socialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Walthamstow, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Hammersmith, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.58,-0.02&amp;year=1874" aria-label="Walthamstow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4550 379 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William Morris"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5000 649h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5000 649h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.58,-0.02&amp;year=1874">What was here in 1874? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Have nothing in your houses that you do not know to be useful or believe to be beautiful.&#8221;</p><footer>The Beauty of Life, lecture of 1880 (Hopes and Fears for Art, 1882) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3773/pg3773-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I do not want art for a few, any more than education for a few, or freedom for a few.&#8221;</p><footer>The Art of the People, lecture of 1879 (Hopes and Fears for Art, 1882) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3773/pg3773-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Yes, surely! and if others can see it as I have seen it, then it may be called a vision rather than a dream.&#8221;</p><footer>News from Nowhere (1890), closing words &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3261/pg3261-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Forsooth, brothers, fellowship is heaven, and lack of fellowship is hell: fellowship is life, and lack of fellowship is death: and the deeds that ye do upon the earth, it is for fellowship's sake that ye do them…&#8221;</p><footer>A Dream of John Ball (1888) &#183; original &#183; spoken by John Ball, the priest of the Peasants' Revolt of 1381 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/357/pg357-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
